==German==

===Alternative forms===
* {{l|de|Triebsand}} {{q|obsolete}}

===Etymology===
From {{inh|de|gmh|tripsant}}, {{m|gmh|trybsand}} (both [[Central German]], 14th c.). Like all High German attestations before the 16th century, these seem to be equivalent to modern {{com|de|Trieb|Sand}}, which is notable because {{cog|gmh|trip}} was commonly used of the driving of livestock only. Hence, possibly from {{der|de|gml|drifsant}} (15th c., could be {{m|gml||drīfsant}}), {{m|gml|dryffsandt}} (16th c.), from {{m|gml||*driftsant}} (compare unambiguous {{cog|nl|drifsant}}, {{m|nl|driftzant}}, 16th c.). A borrowing from Low German may also be corroborated by the semantic context, though neither silt nor quicksand are restricted to seashores. The modern form with ''-ei-'' by adaptation to underlying {{m|de|treiben}} (also in contemporary {{cog|nl|drijfzand}}, {{cog|nds|Driefsand}}).

